data.content:
Your code **doesn't use `Thread.Abort`** — it simply spawns threads, runs work, and joins them. This will work on .NET 8 as-is.

However, the pattern itself is outdated. The modern .NET 8 equivalent using `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task`:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, item =>
        {
            item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
        });
    }
}
```

**If you do have `Thread.Abort` elsewhere**, here's the migration path:

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` + cooperative cancellation |
| `ThreadAbortException` | `OperationCanceledException` |
| Timeout-based abort | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter()` |

**Example with cancellation:**

```csharp
public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken ct)
{
    await Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, ct, async (item, token) =>
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    });
}
```

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 8 — any code calling it will crash at runtime. Search your full codebase with a pattern like `\.Abort\(` to find all instances.
